  (W, "Segwit addresses are the addresses written for segregated witness outputs. They are the answer of the Bitcoin developers to a practical question that the legacy addresses could not answer: how can a payer be given a short, easily typed string that works for the new kinds of output of a protocol upgrade and for kinds that have not yet been invented. The book presents two closely related formats, bech32 for the first version of the upgrade and bech32m for later versions, and says that bech32m is the current recommended address format for Bitcoin wallets [BK]."),
  (Y, "The upgrade in question, called segregated witness or segwit, was introduced in 2017 according to the book, and, when it is used, it prevents transaction identifiers from being changed without the consent of the spender; it also provided additional capacity for transaction data in blocks and several other benefits [BK]. Users who wanted direct access to its benefits had to accept payments to new output scripts, and that demanded a new way of telling payers what to pay. The book lists four problems of the older base58check addresses that the designers wanted to solve at the same time, among them the difficulty of reading them aloud and the need to upgrade every payer's wallet whenever a new protocol feature appeared [BK]."),
