  (W, "Segregated witness, shortened to segwit, is an upgrade of the Bitcoin protocol that defines a new structure called the witness, committed to blocks separately from the tree of transactions. The structure contains the data required to check that a transaction is valid, but not the data that determines what the transaction does; in particular, scripts and signatures are moved into it [B141]. The book dates the upgrade to 2017 [BK], and the specification, BIP141, has the status Deployed [B141]. Chapter 3 introduced the same upgrade from the side of the node, listing segregated witness and the transaction identifiers among the data that a node handles."),
  (Y, "The first motivation that the specification gives is the end of unintentional malleability. Because signature data is no longer part of the transaction hash, changes to how the transaction was signed are no longer relevant to transaction identification [B141]. The book describes the same benefit: when the upgrade is used, the transaction identifier cannot be changed without the consent of the spending user, or of the quorum of signers when several signatures are required [BK]. Transaction identifiers that are stable are a prerequisite for chains of unconfirmed transactions, and the specification mentions that this is important for off-chain protocols such as the Lightning Network [B141]."),
  (E, "Segwit is met whenever an address begins with bc1, and it is the reason why a transaction has two identifiers. The txid remains the double SHA256 of the traditional serialisation, while a new identifier, the wtxid, is the double SHA256 of the serialisation that includes the witness [B141]. The worked example shows the consequence on two short byte strings: hashing the bytes of a transaction without its witness and hashing the same bytes with the witness appended give two completely different identifiers, which is why the witness had to be left out of the first of them. The upgrade was designed to be a soft fork, compatible with nodes that did not upgrade, and it did not make older address kinds invalid; it also gave blocks additional capacity for transaction data, as the book says [BK]."),
  (H, "The specification sets out the mechanism. Each transaction input is associated with a witness field, a list of stack items, and for an input that does not spend a witness program the field is empty, a single zero byte. The witnesses of a block are committed in a second tree whose root is recorded in an output of the coinbase transaction, the first transaction of a block, so that the commitment is nested in the block's existing structure, which is what makes the change compatible with a soft fork [B141]. The chapter's checks read the specification and confirm that it defines the two identifiers and the empty witness field in these words."),
  (K, "A frequent error is to think that segwit hides signatures or removes them. They are transmitted and verified as before; they are only stored in a different place and not counted in the identifier. The specification states that transmission of signature data becomes optional for a peer that merely wants to check that a transaction exists [B141], and that is a saving, not a removal of verification. A second error is to think that using a segwit address makes a transaction private: the structure of the transaction remains visible."),
