  (W, "Segwit inside P2SH, often called nested segwit, is the way in which a receiver can use segregated witness through an ordinary P2SH address. The redeem script that the address commits to is the segwit output script itself: the byte for version 0 followed by a push of the 20-byte hash of the public key. The book explains the idea: as the P2SH output type had the advantage that a spender does not need to know the details of the script the receiver uses, the segwit upgrade was designed to use that mechanism, allowing users to begin accessing many of its benefits immediately through a P2SH address [BK]."),
  (Y, "Nested segwit was a bridge. A receiver wanting segwit did not have to wait for all payers to upgrade, because every wallet that could pay a P2SH address could pay this one. The book adds the limit of the bridge: to gain access to all the benefits, Bob would need Alice's wallet to pay him with a different type of script, and that would require Alice's wallet to upgrade to support the new scripts [BK]. The bech32 address was the way out of the dilemma."),
  (E, "The nested form is still visible in Bitcoin Core, whose address type options are legacy, p2sh-segwit, bech32 and bech32m [CORE]. The documentation of descriptors calls the corresponding output a P2SH-P2WPKH output and writes it sh(wpkh(KEY)) [CORE]. Such an address begins with 3 like every P2SH address, so it cannot be told from a multisignature P2SH address by looking at it."),
