  (W, "The witness version is a number between 0 and 16 that tells which set of rules governs a witness output. In a bech32m address it is a single byte that is encoded as a single character immediately following the separator. The book says that the letter q is the encoding of 0 for segwit version 0, the initial version, where bech32 addresses were introduced, and the letter p is the encoding of 1 for segwit version 1, also called taproot, where bech32m began to be used [BK]. There are seventeen possible versions, and Bitcoin requires that the first byte of the data part decode to a number from 0 to 16 inclusive [BK]."),
  (Y, "The version is the means by which the protocol can grow. A new kind of output receives a new version number, and wallets that can pay any version can pay it without a new address format. The designers' stated hope was that developers could build a wallet today that allows spending to a bech32 address and have that wallet remain able to pay bech32 addresses for users of new features added in future protocol upgrades [BK]. That is why the book asks implementers to pass the test vectors concerning the versions that are not yet defined."),
  (E, "The characters follow from the alphabet: q is the character at position 0 and p at position 1, and the book's last example, an address for the highest version, begins with bc1s because s is at position 16 [BK]. The chapter's checks look the positions up in the alphabet of the specification. In the output script the version is stored as an opcode: version 0 is OP_0, which uses the byte 0x00, but version 1 uses OP_1, which is the byte 0x51, and the versions 2 to 16 use 0x52 to 0x60 [BK]."),
